  "raw": {
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Adjunct%20Faculty_Constantin-Braniff_11.2025.pdf": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Various Courses, Constantin College and Braniff Graduate School of Liberal Arts",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor, Various Courses, Constantin College and Braniff Graduate School of Liberal Arts.  \n \n Adjunct Instructor                                            Job Number: 2161UD  \nConstantin College and Braniff Graduate School of Liberal Arts  \n  \nSUMMARY \n \nAdjunct faculty to teach courses in the academic departments of Art, Biology, Chemistry, Classics, Drama, \nEconomics, Education, English, History, Math, Modern Languages, Philosophy, Physics, Politics, \nPsychology, and Theology.  \n \nWe are particularly seeking to hire faculty to teach in the following areas. The courses listed under a \ngiven area will likely be in particular need of faculty. \n \nBiology \n• Laboratory courses in Anatomy \n• Laboratory courses in Physiology \n• Laboratory courses in Microbiology \n \nChemistry \n• Organic Chemistry I and II \n• Laboratory courses in Organic Chemistry \n \nClassics \n• First- or second-year courses in Latin \n• First- or second-year courses in Greek \n \nEnglish \n• ENG 1301: Literary Tradition I \n• ENG 1302: Literary Tradition II \n \nHistory \n• HIS 1312 American Civilization II \n• HIS 2302 Western Civilization II \n \nMath \n• MAT 1303 Precalculus \n \nPhysics \n• General Physics I and II \n• Laboratory courses in General Physics  \n \nPolitics \n• POL 1311: Principles of American Politics  \n \n \n \nSpanish \n• MSP 1301 First Year Spanish I \n• MSP 1302 First Year Spanish II \n• MSP 2311 Second Year Spanish I \n \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES \n \n• Instruct undergraduate and/or graduate students. \n• Invite, facilitate, and moderate classroom discussion. \n• Evaluate and grade student coursework. \n• Maintain student attendance records, grades, and other required records. \n• Communicate with students to provide necessary guidance and assistance. \n• Participate in meetings pertinent to faculty responsibilities. \n \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS \n \n• Master’s degree with at least 18 graduate credit hours in teaching area (for teaching \nundergraduate-level courses). \n• Earned doctorate degree in disciplinary field (for teaching graduate-level courses). \n \n \nWHO WE ARE \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \n \nAPPLICATION \n \nAll applications should be submitted online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/  \n   \n \n  \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \n \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Adjunct%20Faculty_COB_09.2025.pdf": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Various Courses, Gupta College of Business",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor, Various Courses, Gupta College of Business.  \n  \n  \nAdjunct Instructor                                        Job Number: 000027UD  \nGupta College of Business  \n  \n \nWHO WE ARE \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY  \n  \nThe University of Dallas’ Gupta College of Business is seeking dynamic adjunct faculty members to \ninstruct assigned undergraduate and/or graduate business courses. We are especially interested in \nprofessionals who are actively working in their fields and eager to bring current industry knowledge, \npractices, and insights into the classroom. Course areas may include: Accounting, Business Analytics, \nBusiness Communications, Business, Ethics, Business law, Business Mathematics & Statistics, \nCybersecurity, Economics, Entrepreneurship, Finance, Global Business, Information Technology \nManagement, Marketing, Management, Operations, Writing Academic Tutor, Finance Academic Tutor, \nand Statistics Academic Tutor.  \n  \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES   \n \n• Prepare and deliver lectures that integrate academic content with real-world, industry-based \napplications. \n• Draw from current professional experiences to facilitate and enrich classroom discussions. \n• Evaluate and grade student coursework in alignment with academic standards. \n• Maintain student attendance records, grades, and other required documentation. \n• Plan, evaluate, and revise course content, materials, and instructional methods to ensure both \nacademic rigor and industry relevance. \n• Maintain regularly scheduled office hours to advise and mentor students. \n• Communicate proactively with students to provide guidance and support. \n• Stay current in both your professional field and in teaching practices through continuous \ndevelopment and engagement. \n• Participate in meetings and collaborative efforts that support the mission of the Gupta College of \nBusiness. \n \n  \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS   \n• Master’s degree from an AACSB-accredited institution (or comparable accreditation specific to the \nfield) with at least 18 graduate credit hours in the teaching area. \n• Significant professional experience in a business-related field, with the ability to integrate industry \nexpertise into teaching.  \n• Experience teaching business-related courses at the undergraduate and/or graduate level.  \n• Excellent oral and written communication skills.  \n• Strong presentation skills and a clear focus on the discipline.  \n• Significant business management skills.  \n• Knowledge and practice of a variety of instructional methods and technology that ensure successful \nstudent learning. Experience with experimental learning.  \n• Ability to work well in a collaborative work environment.  \n• Demonstrated commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and student success.  \n  \n PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS  \n• Currently employed in a business, finance, technology, or management role that directly relates \nto course content. \n• Teaching experience in either online or face to face format.  \n  \n \nAPPLICATION \nAll applications should be submitted online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/  \n  \n  \n  \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \n \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. \n "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Admission%20Counselor_08.2026.pdf": {
      "title": "Admission Counselor",
      "description": "Admission Counselor.  \n \n                                                                                      Job Number: 16699UD \nAdmission Counselor                                                         Date Posted: August 24, 2026 \n \nWHO WE ARE  \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking an Admission Counselor to recruit qualified students for admission and \nenrollment to the University of Dallas within assigned territory. The Admission Counselor will \ntravel to off-campus recruitment events, evaluate application files for admission decisions, \nmeet with students in person, conduct on-campus admission presentations to groups of \nstudents/ parents, and contact students and parents via phone, email and mail. This position \nwill communicate effectively on the value of a UD education, the technical nature of the \nfinancial aid process, the merits of specific majors and concentrations, and the traditions that \nhelp build UD’s unique community. \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES  \n \n• Nurture, build, and sustain ongoing professional relationships with prospective students \nand those who influence them to achieve assigned enrollment management objectives. \n• Become familiar with the college admission process, including but not limited to sources \nof students, preferences of students, and sources of competition. Learn and use what \nworks best for achievement of enrollment management objectives.  \n• Research the potential for travel and recruiting programs, develop a plan for off-campus \nactivities to achieve enrollment management objectives most effectively, present this \nplan for the Director’s approval, and then execute the approved plan within the \napproved budget. \n• Cultivate alumni, parents, college counselors, and other constituencies to assist with the \nrecruitment process. \n• Monitor the inquiry, prospect, application, and deposit numbers to focus on attaining \nassigned objectives. \n• Determine the best next source and means of institutional contact for prospects. \n \n• Re-qualify the inquiry pool, so that those inquiries most likely to enroll are the ones \nbeing contacted on a regular basis. \n• Monitor the receipt of applications and determine the best next steps in the \nrecruitment process for each applicant. \n• Ensure that all inbound and outbound communication with prospects and applicants is \nrecorded using office protocol. \n• Evaluate applications for admission and make each admit/deny decision using \ndiscretionary professional judgment about the applicant’s academic preparation, \nacademic work ethic, and fit for the academic programs at the University of Dallas. \n• Document activity and decisions about applications for admission in accordance with \nestablished standards. \n• Communicate with prospective students about financial aid, financial resources, and \nhow to pay for college. \n• Other duties as assigned to ensure the efficient and effective operation of the Office of \nUndergraduate Admission. \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS  \n• Bachelor’s degree from an accredited university. \n \nADDITIONAL INFORMATION \n \n• Supervise, train, mentor, and evaluate the work of one or more part-time marketing \ninterns. \n• The position requires travel within assigned territory for 10-12 weeks in a calendar year, \nand the ability to work evenings, partial weekends, and campus visit programs as \nneeded for success. \n• The work requires a valid Texas driver’s license with a favorable driving record. \nBENEFITS  \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions.  \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Assistant%20Controller_09.2026.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Controller",
      "description": "Assistant Controller.  \n \n                                                                                      Job Number: 17218UD \nAssistant Controller                                                    Date Posted: September 15, 2026  \n \nWHO WE ARE  \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking an Assistant Controller to lead all day-to-day finance operations of the \nUniversity and supervise a team, including functional responsibility over accounts payable, \npayroll, and financial accounting. The Assistant Controller is responsible for overall \nmanagement of accounting operations, including general ledger, accounts payable, \nendowment, and grants. The Assistant Controller will ensure that the University has the \nsystems and procedures in place to support effective and efficient financial operations \nconducted within an established framework of internal controls. \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES  \n \n• Prepare, examine, and analyze accounting records, financial statements, and other \nfinancial reports to assess accuracy, completeness, and conformance to reporting and \nprocedural standards. \n• Ensure timely closing of all financial accounting records on a monthly, quarterly, and \nannual basis. \n• In collaboration with the Controller, establish and maintain financial controls to monitor \nand protect the University’s resources, including, but not limited to, ensuring that \nappropriate account reconciliations are performed, documented, and reviewed on a \ntimely basis. \n• Perform general ledger review to note abnormal balances or transactions, researching, \ninitiating and approving corrective actions. \n• Review and approve the accounts payable processing for accuracy and ensure full \ncompliance with University policies and procedures.  \n• Ensure that all records related to Development and University Relations agree to the \ngeneral ledger and are properly recorded, in agreement with donor documentation.   \n \n• Recommend and implement changes to operational policies and procedures. Analyze, \ndocument, and design all business office processes and the recommendation of \nimprovement initiatives, maximizing value and minimizing waste. \n• Manage financial aid and grant reporting, including monthly reconciliations, invoicing, \nand preparation of annual reports, FISAP, and Single Audits. \n• Ensure compliance with local, state, and federal financial reporting requirements. \nOversee development of reports and surveys, as required by regulatory agencies. \n• Prepare annual financial statements and documents in support of the annual \nindependent audit and other audit activities.   \n• Serve as liaison for all audit work, including coordination with departments outside the \nOffice of Finance \n• Oversee the preparation of the documents related to the following tax forms – Form 990, \nForm 990-T and FBAR. \n• Other duties as assigned to ensure the efficient and effective operation of the Finance \nDepartment. \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS  \n• Bachelor’s degree in accounting or finance. \n• Minimum of 5 years of increasingly responsible accounting experience.  \nPREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \n• Master’s or CPA. \n• Experience in not-for-profit.  \nADDITIONAL INFORMATION \n \n• Directly supervises the following positions:   Staff Accountants \n \nBENEFITS  \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions.   \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. \n "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Campus%20Minister_09.2026M.pdf": {
      "title": "Campus Minister",
      "description": "Campus Minister.  \n \n                                                                                      Job Number: 17238UD \nCampus Minister                                                    Date Posted: September 15, 2026 \n \nWHO WE ARE  \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking a campus minister who will provide both spiritual and academic \nformation in the Catholic faith through faith formation programming and service opportunities, \nas well as interpersonal accompaniment of the University of Dallas student body throughout \nthe academic year. In particular, this position will be responsible for ministering to male \nstudents. \n \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES  \n \nAccompaniment / Spiritual Direction \n• Provide spiritual advice to current students, faculty, and staff (ongoing and as needed). \n• Minister to, care for, walk with, listen to, and pray with students in need. \n• Coordinate care with the counseling department, CARE team, OSA and other departments as \nneeded. \n \nMinistry Support \nIn collaboration with other campus ministers: \n• Create curriculum for weekly discussion group/faith formation community with assistance from \nthe Director of Campus Ministry. \n• Form student men as peer leaders within community of UD Men’s Society or Knights of \nColumbus. \n• Give frequent talks throughout the year; advertise and execute all events for the ministry. \n• Manage all administrative work that this duty entails. \n \n \n \nRetreats \nIn collaboration with other campus ministers: \n• Design retreat programs to meet the established objectives of the Genesis, Winter, and Exodus \nretreats. \n• Vet, select and empower student leaders to direct and staff peer-led Campus Ministry retreats. \n• Give talks as required. \n• Coordinate and provide oversight of travel. \n• Manage related administrative duties. \n \nLiturgical and Sacramental Support \nIf assigned: \n• Provide liturgical support for Sunday and daily Masses as well as weddings, funerals and special \nevents, as assigned.  \n• Planning, coordination, and communication of liturgies to necessary parties; preparing Mass \nintention list for celebrant and the prayers of the faithful.  \n• Recruiting, training, and scheduling students to assist as lectors, altar servers, sacristans, and \nextraordinary ministers of Holy Communion;  Filling these roles in the absence of students. \n• Coordinating monthly schedule of priests (and deacons) to cover all Masses and confessions. \n \nOutreach/Mission Support \nIf assigned: \n• Coordinate monthly community service outings with area non-profits, churches, care centers, \netc. \n• Select Alternative Spring Break (ASB) team through application and interview process; oversee \nand implement fundraising activities; lead faith formation and development sessions for ASB \nteam prior to mission trip; serve as lead minister on mission trip; manage all administrative work \nthat this duty entails. \n• Advertise events; coordinate student registration for events; transport and participate at events; \nmanage all administrative work that this duty entails. \n \nStudent Club Advisor/Liaison  \nIf assigned: \n• Oversee campus clubs’ activities (those that fall under Campus Ministry); attend certain club \nevents; act as chaperone for at least one weekend trip per year for pro-life club; provide \noperational/spiritual guidance. \n \n \n \n \nAdministrative Support \nIf assigned \n• Provide administrative support to the Chaplain and Director of Campus Ministry. \n• Update website as requested. \n \nOther duties as assigned to ensure the efficient and effective operation of the Office of Campus Ministry \nand the Church of the Incarnation. \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS  \n• Bachelor’s degree. \n• An understanding of and commitment to the values and objectives of Catholic liberal \neducation and the ability to effectively communicate with academically talented \ncollege students.  \n• Understanding of Catholic Social Teaching and the ability to teach it to others.  \n• Strong ability to listen and advise young adults in difficult situations (spiritual \naccompaniment, human formation).  \n• Strong team building and leadership development skills.  \n• High level of organizational skills and interpersonal skills.  \n• Proven ability in planning and implementing programs, equipping volunteer staff, \nand working with community organizations to the broader community.  \n• Proficiency in Google Workspace applications (Google Docs, Sheets, Slides Drive and \nForms) and other online software and apps (Calendly, Canva, Instagram/Facebook) \nthat could be used for advertisement purposes.  \n• Ability to work in both on a team and independently. \n \nPREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \n \n• Bachelor’s degree in theology, religious studies, or pastoral ministry. \n• One to two years of experience in a college campus ministry, parish young adult, or \ncomparable setting. \n• Due to the nature of this role and its focus on managing the Men’s Ministry, male \napplicants are strongly preferred. \n \nADDITIONAL INFORMATION \n \n• This job requires the ability to work irregular work hours, including evenings, weekends, and \nschool holidays and often on short notice. Flexible schedule will be worked out with the Director \nof Campus Ministry to allow for alternate time off. \n \n \n \nBENEFITS  \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions.  \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. \n "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Development%20Project%20Manager_08.2026.pdf": {
      "title": "Development Project Manager",
      "description": "Development Project Manager.  \n \nJob Number: 17138UD \nDevelopment Project Manager Date Posted: August 24, 2026 \n \nWHO WE ARE \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking a Development Project Manager who will be responsible for supporting \nthe implementation of relational giving cycle strategies for development network growth and \ncultivation that fosters long term donor relationships and helps achieve short- and long-term \nfundraising plans through annual restricted and unrestricted support. The Development Project \nManager coordinates the implementation of development projects, executes the distribution of \ncontent for the network, and oversees the distribution of particular engagement to segmented \ndonor groups. This role manages the implementation of student interns’ personalized outreach \nto members of the network, maintains relationships across the university, and directly solicits \ngifts during campaigns. The Development Project Manager reports to the Assistant Vice \nPresident and supports the Development Network Team, particularly with content research, \ncontent distribution, and engagement. This position will apply key principles and best practices \nof higher education advancement, and the role is vital to engaging, retaining, and growing \nnetwork donors. \n \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES \n \n● Development Projects: Manage development network projects to ensure efficient \nproduction, team coordination, and project momentum that delivers both quality and \ntimeliness.  \n● Distribution of Development Content to the Network: Distribute approved content across \ndifferent communication avenues (digital and mail). \n● Distribute Content to Particular Donor Groups: Oversee the distribution of particular \nengagement to segmented donor groups based on a defined cadence for different donor \ngroups (set by the AVP) and attend corresponding donor group events. \n● Oversee Student Intern Outreach: Manage the implementation of student interns’ \npersonalized engagement to members of the network via phone, email, mail, and/or in \nperson events. \n● Maintain Relationships Across the University: Collaborate with faculty, staff, and students \nto gather particular research and information corresponding to development network \n\ncontent projects, build involvement and ownership, and provide a clear line of sight to \nparticular impacts of donor gifts. \n● Directly solicits gifts during campaigns: As part of the Development Network team, \nmembers contact individuals (primarily via phone calls) during campaigns to solicit gifts as \na means to reach the overall campaign fundraising goals. \n● Other duties as assigned to ensure the efficient and effective operation of the \nDevelopment and University Relations Department. \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS \n● Bachelor’s degree.  \n● One to three years of development experience.  \n● Superior communication and interpersonal skills, creativity, political astuteness, and tact. \n \nPREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \n● Experience working in higher education and/or foundations and sponsored research. \n● Demonstrated previous experience interacting with high-level donors and managing a \nteam of volunteers and/or students. \n● Experience with Slate.  \n \nADDITIONAL INFORMATION    \n \n• Occasional evening or weekend work in support of the Department of Development and \nUniversity Relations.  \n• Manage student workers. \n \nBENEFITS \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions. \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber. \n \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Business%20Process%20Analyst.pdf": {
      "title": "Business Process Analyst",
      "description": "Business Process Analyst.  \n \n                                                                                   Job Number: 17344UD \nDate Posted: October 27, 2026 \nBusiness Process Analyst- Enrollment Systems & Data Integration                                             \n \nWHO WE ARE  \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking a Business Process Analyst who will serve as Enrollment Management’s \nhands-on partner for process discovery, business requirements, workflow and business-rule \ndocumentation, user acceptance testing (UAT), implementation support, and knowledge \ntransfer. Reporting to the Director of Enrollment Systems & Data Integration, the Analyst works \nwith undergraduate and graduate recruitment, Financial Aid, Prospect Management, and \ncampus partners to identify root causes, simplify work, reduce avoidable rework, and verify \nthat approved changes improve daily operations.  \n \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES  \nProcess Discovery and Improvement \n• Observe and interview employees to document current workflows, handoffs, \nexceptions, seasonal volume and recurring enrollment cycles (recruitment launches, \napplication review, admission decisions, aid coordination, yield, orientation, enrollment \nconfirmation, census, and melt prevention), manual workarounds, service expectations, \nand downstream effects; use swim lanes, process maps, decision trees, or comparable \nmethods as appropriate. \n• Separate symptoms from root causes involving policy, decision rights, staffing, training, \ndata quality, system configuration, integrations, vendors, or process design. Where \npractical, measure cycle time, queue age, error/rework rates, workload, and service \ndelays. \n \n• Recommend simpler, transferable future-state processes, distinguishing appropriate \nautomation from work requiring professional judgment or personal relationships. \nRequirements, Rules, and Decision Rights \n• Elicit and document business needs, functional requirements, approved business rules, \nnormal and exception paths, data/field needs, acceptance criteria, dependencies, \nintended results, and unresolved questions for systems, reporting, integrations, and \nworkflow changes. \n• Distinguish a business requirement from a proposed technical solution; translate user \nneeds into usable process diagrams, functional specifications, user stories, issue \nstatements, data requirements, and testable criteria for the Director, IT, or vendors. \n• Identify the role with business decision authority and required consultations or \napprovals; surface conflicting rules and undefined authority rather than embedding \nassumptions into configuration or automation. \nTesting, Implementation, and Verification \n• Draft comprehensive UAT and regression test cases across typical transactions, \nexceptions, permissions, communications, reports, data integrity, and downstream \ncross-system consequences, including AI-enabled workflows where applicable. \nCoordinate testing with affected employees and designated functional leaders; validate \npractical usability, document AI limitations and cases requiring human judgment, record \ndefects, distinguish defects from change requests, and track dispositions. \n• Confirm documented requirements and user-facing outcomes before business signoff; \nsupport readiness through training, instructions, assigned responsibilities, support \nchannels, and documented limitations.  \n• After launch, observe real usage and measure adoption, cycle-time improvements, error \nand rework reduction, service impact, and other agreed outcomes; identify unintended \neffects, document realized benefits and remaining friction, and recommend stabilization \nor further refinement. \nData Quality, Documentation, and Knowledge Transfer \n• Use approved CRM/SIS reports, spreadsheets, queries, SQL or comparable tools within \nassigned permissions to validate records, investigate anomalies, reconcile counts, and \ntest workflows.  \n• Work with the Director on validation for imports, mappings, reports, and integrations; \ndocument exceptions and route production defects to the responsible technical \nauthority. Help establish repeatable controls to flag missing, stale, duplicate, \ninconsistent, or out-of-sequence records; document relevant data lineage, limitations, \n \nand assigned data responsibilities. Escalate conflicting data definitions or authoritative-\nsource questions. \n• Maintain accessible process maps, SOPs, requirements, business-rule records, checklists, \ntest scripts, defect and decision logs, job aids, and training materials covering normal \nwork and meaningful exceptions. Develop or deliver practical employee training on \nrevised steps, exceptions, data-quality expectations, and escalation paths; follow up on \nearly confusion or workarounds. Support cross-training and continuity. \nIntake, Coordination, Service, and Governance \n• Assist the Director with transparent request intake and project tracking: responsible \nbusiness lead, problem, impact, urgency, scope, dependencies, risk, current \nworkaround, priority, capacity constraints, assigned leads, required monitoring, \noperational handoffs, issues, status, and next decision. Coordinate business-side \nrequirements, testing, defect resolution, and implementation follow-through with \nvendors and technical partners; escalate competing priorities, scope changes, or stalled \ndecisions to the Director. \n• Facilitate efficient workshops, testing, and implementation activities without creating an \nunnecessary approval layer. Explain findings and tradeoffs clearly, respect employee \nknowledge, and distinguish facts, employee reports, inference, and unverified \nhypotheses. \n• Use minimum-necessary access and handle applicant, student, employee, and \ninstitutional information carefully. Work within FERPA, privacy, approved access, \ninformation-security, change-control, records, and procurement requirements. \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS  \n• Bachelor’s degree in a relevant field.  \n• Five or more years of progressively responsible business, process, or systems analysis \nexperience, including requirements development, complex workflow improvement, \nUAT, and implementation support.  \n• Demonstrated meaningful participation in at least one end-to-end enterprise workflow \nor systems implementation, from discovery and requirements through testing, launch, \nand adoption.  \n• Experience mapping processes, diagnosing root causes, documenting business rules, \ncoordinating complex UAT and regression testing, and demonstrating measurable \noperational improvement. \n• Practical data literacy for reconciliation, structured data analysis, exception \ninvestigation, relational-data concepts, and query/reporting tools. \n \n• Strong written communication, facilitation, and analytical judgment skills, with the \nability to collaborate effectively with vendors and bridge the gap between front-line \nusers and technical teams. \n \nPREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \n \n• Experience with process mapping (e.g., Visio, Lucidchart, Lean/Six Sigma) \n• Experience with work/requirements tracking (e.g., Jira, Confluence, ServiceNow) \n• SQL skills, including basic joins and validation queries and Excel/BI tools \n• Business-side data mapping, integration or ETL/ELT projects \n• Regulated data or documented change controls \n• Experience using Slate and Banner  \n \n \nBENEFITS  \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions.   \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. \n "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Director%20of%20Enrollment%20Prospect%20Management.pdf": {
      "title": "Director of Enrollment Prospect Management",
      "description": "Director of Enrollment Prospect Management.  \n \n                                                                                   Job Number: 17342UD \nDate Posted: October 27, 2026 \nDirector of Enrollment Prospect Management \n \nWHO WE ARE  \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking a Director of Enrollment Prospect Management who will lead an \nenrollment-wide service that helps undergraduate and graduate recruiters better understand \nprospective students, prioritize their attention and make sound next moved. The Director \ntranslates student-initiated behavior, recruiter observations, validated enrollment evidence, \nand CRM information into useful methods, training, coaching, and recruiter tools. \n \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES  \nProspect Strategy, Prioritization, and Funnel Insight \n• Build and improve practical prospect-management methods for undergraduate and \ngraduate recruitment, tailored to differing student populations, academic programs, \nmarkets, and decision processes. Translate Vice President of Enrollment Management \n(VEPM) priorities into actionable opportunities for recruiters. \n• Help recruiters identify high priority, stalled, promising, or underserved prospects and \ndetermine useful next actions. Give particular weight to student-initiated engagement; \ntreat scores and predictive indicators as aids to, rather than substitutes for, recruiter \njudgment. \n• Interpret validated movement from prospect/inquiry through application, completion, \nadmission, commitment or deposit, enrollment, and melt prevention. Identify \nconversion gaps, unusual behavior, and actionable patterns; distinguish the validated \nanalytical finding, field observation, inference, and recommendation. \n \n• Define the business question and recruiter use case before requesting technical scoring, \nsegment identification, priority queues, territory views, reports, dashboards, or alerts \nfrom Systems & Data Integration. Work with the technical team to assess evidence \nquality and limitations.  \n• When findings warrant a scalable intervention, communicate the behavioral insight and \nrecruiter need to Campaigns & Demand Generation, which develops and executes the \ncampaign response within VPEM-approved messaging, channel, budget, and campaign \npriorities. \nRecruiter Coaching, Ethical Counseling, and Individual Communication \n• Partner with undergraduate and graduate recruitment leaders to strengthen recruiter \ncapability through Prospect Management training, hands-on coaching, office hours, \npeer learning, job aids, and practical feedback. Coordinate onboarding and coaching \nactivities with the responsible recruitment leader, who retains authority for employee \nexpectations, individual development plans, performance evaluation, and day-to-day \nrecruitment direction. Build shared CRM and data fluency through documented \nmethods and cross-training so knowledge survives staff transitions. \n• Teach purposeful conversations about prospective students’ goals, abilities, interests, \nconcerns, competing options, decision factors, affordability and financial pressures, and \ncommunication preferences.  \n• Help recruiters customize rather than merely personalize one-to-one follow-up using \napproved information, timing, stage, channels, questions, and student response. Protect \nrecruiter stewardship of each relationship; keep communications focused and suited to \nstudent preference and need. \n• Frame recruitment as truthful, student-centered directive counseling that helps \nstudents decide whether UD fits. When a recruiter struggles, help the line supervisor \ndiagnose expectations, training, processes, data, systems, workload, market factors, and \nperformance. \nRequirements, CRM Partnership, and Practical Implementation \n• Establish and maintain prospect-management methods and functional recruiter use \ncases; document requirements for counselor-of-record assignment and responsibility, \nstages, engagement signals, approved data fields, communication preferences, activity \nlogging, queues, dashboards, alerts, automations, and recruiter workflows.  \n• Identify and articulate recruiter problems and functional use cases. Partner with the \nBusiness Process Analyst to investigate underlying workflows, root causes, \nrequirements, and alternatives; work with Systems & Data Integration and designated \n \ndata stewards to validate business rules, technical feasibility, measurement, and \nreliability.  \n• Participate in design review, user acceptance testing, launch preparation, employee \ntraining, and post-launch validation; verify tools improve real recruiter work and remain \ndocumented, transferable, and understandable beyond one person. \n• Establish and maintain practical, Enrollment-wide methods and standards for timely, \naccurate documentation of meaningful prospective-student interactions in approved \nsystems, within VPEM-established data governance. Partner with undergraduate and \ngraduate recruitment leaders to implement these standards through training, clear \nexpectations, and useful tools.  \nIndividualized Resources and University Partnerships \n• Maintain a current, readily accessible inventory of approved recruitment content, \ncredible evidence, knowledgeable University contacts, and appropriate delivery \nmethods that counselors can use immediately in individualized student and family \nconversations. Organize resources by student interest, question, concern, academic \nprogram, decision factor, and enrollment stage. Enable counselors to select and deploy \nappropriate resources within established University guidelines, exercising their \nprofessional judgment and stewardship of the prospective-student relationship.  \n• Coordinate with VPEM, Marketing & Communications, academic leaders, and \nresponsible institutional authorities for factual accuracy, appropriate permissions, brand \ncompliance, currency, and approved use.  \n• Distinguish 1:1 recruiter guidance, response interpretation, and individualized channels \nfrom scalable campaign messaging, population communication, sequencing, campaign-\ncalendar planning and execution, and campaign optimization, which belong to \nCampaigns & Demand Generation. \n• Improve handoffs when students need authoritative academic, financial-aid, visit, \ninternational, registrar, or other specialized guidance. Identify and articulate recurring \nrecruiter-facing friction and its effects on students and staff; refer workflow and root-\ncause investigation to the Business Process Analyst and coordinate with the responsible \nleaders on approved remedies.  \nService, Measurement, and Responsible Governance \n• Maintain accessible service intake, documented request assignments, status, and \nresponse for recruiter requests.  \n• Measure adoption and practical value using recruiter usability, follow-up timeliness, \ndata completeness, prioritization, queue movement, prospect behavior, individualized \nchannel response, conversion patterns and training effectiveness.  \n \n• Report material findings, risks, service needs, and recommended action to the VPEM \nand recruitment leaders. Avoid mistaking volume for impact or attributing recruiter \noutcomes solely to individuals when systems, capacity, policy, or markets materially \ncontributed.  \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS  \n• Bachelor’s degree. \n• Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in higher education \nadmission, undergraduate or graduate recruitment, enrollment management, \nenrollment operations, CRM-enabled recruitment, or closely related work.   \n• Substantial direct experience working with prospective students and recruitment \nfunnels through recruitment or closely leading recruiters.  \n• Practical mastery of prospect prioritization, enrollment funnel and student decision \nprocesses.  \n• Validated data interpretation with appropriate uncertainty.  \n• Cross functional implementation and change leadership. \n \nPREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \n \n• Experience across different recruitment populations or programs.  \n• CRM experience and Banner or another SIS. \n• Demonstrated expertise with prospect/funnel management, coaching, CRM workflows, \nanalytics, segmentation, communications, or operational improvement.  \n \nADDITIONAL INFORMATION \n \nSome travel and occasional evening/weekend work may be required.  \n \nBENEFITS  \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions.   \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Director%20of%20Enrollment%20Systems%20and%20Data%20Integration.pdf": {
      "title": "Director of Enrollment Systems and Data Integration",
      "description": "Director of Enrollment Systems and Data Integration.  \n \n                                                                                                          Job Number: 17343UD \nDirector of Enrollment Systems & Data Integration                        Date Posted: October 27, 2026 \n \nWHO WE ARE  \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking a Director of Enrollment Systems & Data Integration to lead Enrollment \nManagement’s systems, production data integration, data operations, operational reporting \nand analytics, and systems-enabled process improvement. This hands-on functional-technical \nleader will maintain assigned production ETL/ELT pipelines and integrations; will administer and \nimprove enrollment CRM/SIS functions; and translate approved business needs into reliable, \nmaintainable tools and workflows. \n \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES  \n \nEnrollment Systems and Operational Reliability \n•  Lead functional administration, configuration, testing, release readiness, and continuous \nimprovement of Slate, enrollment-related Banner processes, and other approved Enrollment \nsystems. \n•  Prepare critical systems and processes for prospecting, recruitment, applications, admission, \nfinancial-aid coordination, yield, orientation, enrollment confirmation, census, and melt \nprevention; diagnose configuration, data, process, integration, user, vendor, and \ninfrastructure failures and coordinate resolution within assigned authority. \n•  Maintain configuration records, release notes, operating checklists, recovery procedures, \nbackup coverage, and cross-training to reduce key-person dependency. \n \nProduction Data Engineering, Integrations, and Advanced SQL \n•  Personally design, develop, test, deploy, monitor, troubleshoot, and maintain production \nETL/ELT pipelines and system-to-system integrations. Use advanced SQL for extraction, \ntransformation, validation, reconciliation, performance analysis, operational reporting, and \nroot-cause investigation. \n \n•  Implement source-to-target mappings, transformations, reference/lookup logic, \ndeduplication, validation, exception handling, and reconciliation. \n•  Choose the simplest supportable integration and automation approach for the approved \nneed. Document material tradeoffs in cost, interoperability, security, reliability, and technical \ndebt. \n•  Apply production controls appropriate to risk. \n•  Remediate integration failures and affected data. Design maintainable solutions rather than \nbrittle customization. Engage enterprise IT or vendors when reserved infrastructure, \ndatabase, security, identity, or privileged access authority is required. \n•  Periodically review assigned integrations, manual dependencies, platform capabilities, \ncustom code, and technical debt. Recommend maintenance, modernization, replacement, or \nretirement of components. \n \nData Operations, Reporting, and Responsible Analysis \n•  Direct and perform assigned Enrollment-layer imports, exports, mapping, validation, \nreconciliation, exception monitoring, data-quality processes, and operational reporting. Build \nreliable queries, dashboards, alerts, extracts, measures, and analytical tools for recruitment, \nadmission, financial-aid coordination, yield, and forecasting. \n•  Implement Vice President for Enrollment Management (VPEM)-approved definitions and \nbusiness rules within University data governance; document data lineage, field use, \ncalculations, report stewardship, limitations, and controls so results are reproducible and \nverifiable. \n•  Identify recurring data errors and repair root causes. When developing segmentation, \nengagement indicators, scoring, or predictive tools, test assumptions and explain what the \nevidence can and cannot support. VPEM retains executive interpretation of enrollment \nperformance and risk. \n \nRequirements, Process Improvement, and Implementation \n•  Maintain visible request intake, scope, assigned lead, dependencies, status, risks, and next \ndecisions. Manage operational queue priorities and technical sequencing within VPEM-\nestablished Enrollment priorities; elevate material resource conflicts and competing strategic \npriorities to the VPEM. Produce and approve technical specifications within delegated \nauthority, and coordinate functional specifications, data mappings, validation criteria, and \nimplementation plans; the designated business authority approves business requirements, \npolicy, and program rules. \n•  Supervise the Business Process Analyst’s (BPA) process discovery, workflow mapping, \nrequirements, business-rule documentation, testing coordination, and implementation \nfollow-through. Review significant analysis and alternatives; retain accountability for \ntechnical design and implementation quality. \n \n \n•  Address manual reentry, unnecessary handoffs, undocumented workarounds, fragile \nspreadsheets and integrations, and avoidable employee burden. Distinguish system failures \nfrom problems in policy, role design, training, staffing, market conditions, or execution; \nmeasure whether changes actually improve operations. \n \nProspect Management, User Enablement, and Campus Service \n•  Turn approved Prospect Management methods and recruiter use cases into measures, \nreports, segments, queues, alerts, and integrations, explaining analytic limitations. Support \nUndergraduate Admission, Gupta, Braniff, Financial Aid, visits, and other units without \ninserting an additional supervisory or business-approval layer. \n•  Train staff in system use, fields, reports, queues, data-entry expectations, and workflows; \nproduce practical user guides, technical runbooks, data dictionaries, test records, process \nmaps, and recurring-cycle documentation.  \n•  Coordinate vendor support and implementation, document service issues, manage assigned \ntechnical/functional vendor relationships, maintain a transparent backlog, surface risks and \ncapacity limits, and close loops with users. Procurement, contracting, signature, and budget \ndecisions remain with designated authorities. Provide vendor service and renewal input, \nincluding assessment of whether purchased capabilities are used effectively. \n \nSecurity, Governance, and Decision Rights \n•  Operate within FERPA, privacy, records, access, data security, contractual, and University \ntechnology standards; document and escalate material compliance, access, data, and \noperational risks. Coordinate permissions and service accounts with the appropriate data \nsteward, access authority, and IT. Document approvals and perform periodic access reviews \nwhere assigned. \n•  This Director will implement authorized rules, identify defects, and recommend changes; the \nDirector does not independently change those rules. Escalate unresolved cross-functional \nconflicts to the VPEM. \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS  \n•  Bachelor’s degree in a related discipline, or substantially equivalent education, technical \ntraining, certifications, and directly relevant experience as permitted by University policy. \n•  Seven or more years of progressively responsible, directly transferable experience in \nenterprise systems, data engineering/integration, ETL/ELT, CRM/ERP administration, \nbusiness systems, analytics engineering, or related work. \n•  Demonstrated personal responsibility for production ETL/ELT pipelines; advanced SQL \n(complex joins, transformations, CTEs, aggregations, data validation, reconciliation, and \ntroubleshooting); and integrations through APIs, secure files, scheduled jobs, middleware, \nconnectors, or comparable methods. \n \n•  Experience with mapping, error handling, monitoring, operational recovery, data quality, \ntesting (integration/regression/UAT), deployment controls, and production support; complex \nenterprise workflow/configuration and process/requirements analysis. \n•  Ability to communicate technical issues to nontechnical users, supervise and coach an \nanalyst, work across organizational boundaries, protect confidential data, prioritize \nrealistically, and build trust through clear, reliable service. \n \nPREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \n• Slate or comparable complex CRM/workflow platform; Banner Student or comparable \nSIS/ERP. Prior university experience is welcome but not required. \n• ETL/ELT and integration tools such as Azure Data Factory, SSIS, Boomi, Informatica, \nMuleSoft, Talend, Matillion, Fivetran, dbt, or equivalents; Python, PowerShell, JavaScript, \nor related scripting. \n• Power BI, Tableau, Argos, or comparable BI; data modeling, lineage, metadata, \ngovernance, cloud integration, API authentication, SFTP, Git/source control, and disciplined \nchange management. \n• CRM configuration/communications technologies such as HTML, CSS, XML, Liquid Markup, \nand related tools; documentation in runbooks, process maps, data dictionaries, \nspecifications, SOPs, and training guides; familiarity with \n \nADDITIONAL INFORMATION \n \n• The Director will directly supervise and develop the Business Process Analyst (BPA), \nworking with undergraduate and graduate recruitment, Financial Aid, Prospect \nManagement, IT, Institutional Research, and other campus partners.  \n• The role combines deep technical capability with practical business-process judgment, \nuser support, documentation, and operational stewardship.  \n \nBENEFITS  \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions.  \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. \n "
    },
    "https://udallas.edu/offices-services/human-resources/_documents_open_positions/JP_Accountant%20I_10.2026.pdf": {
      "title": "Accountant I",
      "description": "Accountant I.  \n \n                                                                                      Job Number: 17382UD \nAccountant I                                       Date Posted: October 30, 2026 \n \nWHO WE ARE  \n \nThe University of Dallas is a premier Catholic liberal arts university that seeks to educate the \nwhole person, encouraging its students to pursue wisdom, truth and virtue as the proper and \nprimary ends of education. All employees are expected to support the university’s Mission, \nwhich is informed by the Apostolic Constitution Ex corde Ecclesiae, the Catholic Church’s \ngoverning document on Catholic universities. The University of Dallas is known for its nationally \nranked Core Curriculum, exceptional post-graduation outcomes, and some of the happiest \nstudents in the country. \n \nSUMMARY \n \nUD is currently seeking an Accountant I who will support the University's accounting and \nfinancial reporting functions by applying accounting principles and analytical skills to ensure the \naccuracy and integrity of financial data. This position will assist with reconciliations, journal \nentries, month-end close processes, cash accounting, audit support, and financial reporting. \nThis role is designed for an emerging accounting professional seeking to build expertise in \nhigher education, nonprofit, funding, and general accounting. The successful candidate will \ndemonstrate strong attention to detail, intellectual curiosity, professionalism, and a \ncommitment to continuous learning. \nPRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES  \n \n• Perform daily cash accounting activities, including reviewing prior-day bank \ntransactions, ensuring activity is accurately and timely recorded in the general ledger, \nand preparing journal entries for transactions not recorded through other systems or \nprocesses. \n• Serve as backup for daily Positive Pay reviews and exception decisions to help safeguard \nUniversity assets and minimize fraud risk. \n• Monitor and manage the Accounting@udallas.edu mailbox, responding to inquiries, \nresolving issues, and routing requests as appropriate. \n• Review and reconcile gift activity from Development and Advancement systems, \nensuring all gifts are deposited to University bank accounts and recorded in accordance \nwith donor intent and applicable accounting standards. \n• Assist the Assistant Controller with the reconciliation of balance sheet accounts, \nincluding the investigation and resolution of reconciling items. \n \n• Support month-end, quarter-end, and year-end close activities by preparing journal \nentries, reconciliations, analyses, and supporting schedules to ensure accurate \naccounting for prepaid expenses, amortization, fixed assets, and capital assets. \n• Utilize approved AI and emerging technology tools to enhance productivity, research, \nanalysis, reporting, and process improvement initiatives while maintaining sound \nprofessional judgment, data security, and confidentiality. \n• Assist with the annual financial statement audit by preparing schedules, supporting \ndocumentation, and responding to auditor requests within assigned areas of \nresponsibility. \n• Support the preparation of federal and state tax filings, including Forms 990, 990-T, and \n1042, as well as related state charitable solicitation filings, by preparing supporting \nschedules and documentation for review. \n• Participate in process improvement initiatives and recommend efficiencies that \nstrengthen internal controls, enhance reporting accuracy, and improve departmental \noperations. \n• Perform other duties and special projects as assigned in support of the Finance \nDepartment and University objectives. \n \nMINIMUM REQUIREMENTS  \n• Bachelor’s degree in accounting. \n• 0-3 years of accounting experience.  \n \nADDITIONAL INFORMATION \n \n• Recent accounting graduates are encouraged to apply. \n \n \nBENEFITS  \n \nUD provides competitive pay and benefits, including eligibility to participate in medical, dental, \nvision, life and disability insurance; employee events; a health and wellness program; a free \nfitness center; competitive leave programs; tuition waiver, tuition exchange for employees and \ntheir families; and matching retirement plan contributions.  \n \nIf this sounds like the job for you, apply online at: https://hr.udallas.edu/apply/ \n \n \nThe University of Dallas seeks to recruit, develop, and retain faculty, staff and administration of the highest caliber.  \nThe University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and encourages applications from individuals who will enhance our \ncommunity and advance our Mission. \n "
    }
  },
